Looking at the actual structure, after the main switch statement there's nothing else in `processCodePoint`, so the return is really just skipping the rest of the switch. I could refactor this by having the helper return a boolean that tells the caller whether to return early. So the key insight is that since there's no code after the switch statement in `processCodePoint`, the `return` in case 27 is functionally equivalent to a `break` — both exit the method the same way. This means extracting case 27 into a helper method is safe; I just call the helper and then `break` to exit the switch, which naturally ends the method.

